high level Carmichael welcome to another  edition of ask Evan today I'm going to  answer question from one of our readers  Max who runs a company called orange  digital marketing and he wrote in with a  question about his company and he wanted  to know how do I manage a fast-growing  company that is taking on a lot of new  employees so thanks to the question max  I'm going to give you three suggestions  that I hope will help hire the right  person one at a time the number one  mistake that most high growth companies  will make its hiring the wrong people  too quickly it makes sense you're  busting at the seams and need to bring  people on board to get all the work done  especially businesses with under ten  employees every new hire is critical  you're hiring should have both the  skills you aren't and the cultural and  values fit that will let them thrive in  your company if they don't meet your  requirements keep searching until you  get the right candidate it's far better  to hire right and have someone thrive in  a position than to be constantly  replacing the position because you  didn't hire properly at the beginning  it's less headache will save you time  and money in the long run and will  provide you with a much better working  culture if you take your time and do it  properly here's an example from Jeff  Bezos from amazon com one of the biggest  factors behind the amazing success of  amazon is the people the company has  behind it from day one Jeff Bezos has  made it his priority to hire very  carefully it might not have been the  company's best use of resources but  Bezos was taking a long-term perspective  he wanted this company to have a long  shelf life I knew that with his stop  you'd be create an enduring culture  according to Bezos I'd rather interview  50 people and not hire anyone that hired  the wrong person ensure new hires get  proper training another big problem with  rapidly growing companies is that they  don't train new employees enough even if  you take your time and hiring the right  person you have to make sure you set  them up for success once they come on  board you want to have high expectations  but not impossible ones make sure you  set in your schedule time to work with  them to explain how you do things  and always be available for questions if  they have them no matter how busy you  are the last thing you want is for your  team to be afraid to approach you with  questions and ideas inadequate training  leads to bad morale a loss of  productivity and ultimately an inferior  product or service being produced if  hiring and training both fall on your  shoulders you might have to hold off on  hiring someone new until the first  person hired is fully trained and ready  to go here's an example from Ray Kroc  from mcdonalds a strong component of  teamwork crock understood that his grown  company could only continue its meteoric  rise up if it had to support an a  dedication of its workers behind it in  the early stages of the business crock  personally took charge of the entire  hiring process once he'd made the  decision to bring someone on board the  McDonald's team crock would give each  and every one of them a badge which a  title of management trainee it didn't  matter what their actual job was crock  wanted every employee to feel valuable  and like an important part of the team  according to crock none of us is as good  as all of us McDonald's is a people  business and that smile on that counter  girls face when she takes you order is a  vital part of our image turn away  customers I hate turning away business  but I can't accept taking on customers  and then not delivering an outstanding  result for them if you're growing beyond  what your team can produce and you're  taking the right approach to hire and  train properly it may mean you can't  take on every new client that comes to  and that's okay there are two positives  here first you'll be able to pick your  clients and only work with the ones that  are more in that of an ideal fit for  your business second if you position it  properly with your potential client it  will just make them want to work with  you even more we all want what we can't  have and they'll be impressed with your  dedication to quality and customer  service chances are when you are ready  for the business they'll still be ready  for you another option here it could be  the subcontract to another company until  you're able to wrap up enough internally  to handle the business if you go this  route make sure that your subcontractors  have the same dedication of outstanding  service and quality that you do the last  thing you want to do is deliver a  substandard experience to your client  here's an example from Debbie fields  from mrs. Fields Cookies fields once  walked into one of her stores to see a  long line of customers waiting to  purchase a huge batch of cookies that  she immediately knew have been over  baked feels was upset she didn't want  those cookies to be sold or for those  customers to associate her name with  over baked cookies fields approached the  manager to figure out what was going on  what do you think of these cookies  fields asked him he was fired by saying  Oh Debbie they're good enough Fields was  upset and immediately threw out all the  cookies good enough she said good enough  never is Fields now had a long line of  hungry customers waiting to buy cookies  that had just been tossed into the  garbage she went over to them and  explained one by one what had happened  we've over baked these cookies she said  and I want them to be perfect and  whatever you're standing in line for I  will absolutely give them to you free  plus all match for free if you will come  back thank you for watching another  edition of ask Evan if you like the  video you want to see more please give  it a thumbs up below what makes you want  to do more of these videos for you guys  I'd also love to hear what you have to  think if you 